<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Barcelona</h1><p class="st-meta">801 CE – 1162 CE · capital Barcelona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Catalan county, whose counts united Catalonia and, through marriage with Aragon in 1137, founded the Crown of Aragon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from the Muslims in 801 after a long siege, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> made it the main county of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marca-hispanica">Marca Hispanica</a>. Its first count, the Goth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bera-count-of-barcelona">Bera</a>, was deposed in 820. In 878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a> received it, and after his death in 897 his heirs inherited it without royal appointment.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts gradually gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>, Osona and other counties into their hands. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 985, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borrell-ii-of-barcelona">Borrell II</a> stopped seeking Frankish protection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-i-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer I</a> is traditionally credited with the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona">Usatges of Barcelona</a>, the core of Catalan law, and bought rights over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer III</a> gained <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> by marriage, and the city prospered through trade and tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1137 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV</a> was betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronilla</a>, heiress of Aragon, and governed both lands. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II</a> succeeded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1162 and in Aragon in 1164, and the county became the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-barcelona.jpg" alt="County of Barcelona" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: HansenBCN · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansi%C3%B3n_peninsular_de_la_Corona_de_Arag%C3%B3n.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>801 CE – 1162 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Barcelona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wilfred the Hairy, Ramon Berenguer I, Ramon Berenguer IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Comtat de Barcelona</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=982&amp;focus=county-of-barcelona" aria-label="County of Barcelona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4733 702 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Barcelona lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5053 958L5015 951M5053 958L4984 945M5053 958L5053 958M5053 958L5053 958M5053 958L5139 672M5053 958L4979 949M5053 958L5068 939M5053 958L5055 941M5053 958L5047 925M5053 958L5066 932"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5015 951h0M4984 945h0M5053 958h0M50